"""Independent lessons following the mathematical order of book sections 19.1–19.4."""
lessons=[];models=[]
def D(*rows):
 lines=[]
 for row in rows:
  if len(row)>85 and r'\quad\text{or}\quad' in row:lines.extend(row.split(r'\quad\text{or}\quad'))
  else:lines.append(row)
 return r'\[\begin{gathered}'+r'\\'.join(lines)+r'\end{gathered}\]'
def ex(code,title,question,solution):
 uid='HYP-E-'+code;models.append(dict(id=uid));return dict(title=title,question=question,solution=solution,verification=uid)
def lesson(key,title,summary,objective,before,ideas,method,mistakes,examples):lessons.append(dict(key=key,id='fp2_hyperbolic_'+key,title=title,summary=summary,objective=objective,before=before,ideas=ideas,method=method,mistakes=mistakes,examples=examples))
base='/courses/maths4u-9231-fp2/topics/fp2_hyperbolic_'
lesson('exponential','Definitions and exponential equations','Build the six functions from exponentials and solve with a positive substitution.',
 'Use exponential definitions, reciprocal relations and all admissible roots.',
 '<p>Review exponentials, logarithms, quadratic equations and exact surds. In particular, an exponential of a real number is always positive.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Two combinations of the same exponentials</h3>'''+D(r'\sinh x=\frac{e^x-e^{-x}}2',r'\cosh x=\frac{e^x+e^{-x}}2',r'\tanh x=\frac{\sinh x}{\cosh x}=\frac{e^{2x}-1}{e^{2x}+1}')+r'''<p>Sinh is odd, cosh is even, and cosh is positive for every real argument. The identity cosh²x−sinh²x=1 follows by subtracting the two squared definitions. Thus cosh x≥1. These functions are not periodic.</p>'''+D(r'\operatorname{sech}x=\frac1{\cosh x}',r'\operatorname{cosech}x=\frac1{\sinh x}',r'\operatorname{coth}x=\frac{\cosh x}{\sinh x}')+r'''<p>Sech is defined for every real x. Cosech and coth exclude x=0 because sinh vanishes there. Inverse-function notation is introduced later; a reciprocal is a different operation.</p><h3>A quadratic whose variable must be positive</h3>'''+D(r'A\cosh x+B\sinh x=C',r'u=e^x>0',r'(A+B)u^2-2Cu+(A-B)=0')+r'''<p>Multiplication by 2u is reversible because u is positive. Solve the polynomial, retain every positive root, then take its logarithm. A zero or negative polynomial root cannot represent eˣ. If A+B=0 the equation becomes linear in u; handle this parameter case separately.</p><h3>A shifted cosh gives a minimum</h3><p>For positive a,b, a eˣ+b e⁻ˣ equals 2√(ab) cosh(x−h), with h=½ln(b/a). Its minimum is 2√(ab), attained only at x=h. Keep the equality case when using this observation.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>State the definitions and any excluded arguments.</li><li>Combine exponentials and set u=eˣ when useful.</li><li>Handle a vanishing leading coefficient before applying a quadratic formula.</li><li>Retain all positive u-values.</li><li>Recover x by logarithms and check in the original relation.</li></ol>',
 ['Hyperbolic functions are not periodic circular functions.','Cosh²x−sinh²x equals 1, with a minus sign.','Cosech and coth exclude zero.','The substitution u=eˣ requires u&gt;0.','Do not divide away a factor that could be zero.'],[
 ex('E1','1 · Exact values without a calculator',r'<p>Find \(\sinh(\ln6)\), \(\cosh(\ln6)\) and \(\tanh(-\ln6)\).</p>',
 D(r'e^{\ln6}=6,\qquad e^{-\ln6}=\frac16',r'\sinh(\ln6)=\frac{35}{12}',r'\cosh(\ln6)=\frac{37}{12}',r'\tanh(-\ln6)=-\frac{35}{37}')+'<p>Use the difference and sum of the exponential pair, then form the ratio. Oddness determines the negative tanh value.</p>'),
 ex('E2','2 · Recover all reciprocal values',r'<p>Given \(\sinh a=\frac8{15}\) and \(a>0\), find cosh, tanh and the three reciprocal functions at a.</p>',
 D(r'\cosh a=\sqrt{1+\frac{64}{225}}=\frac{17}{15}',r'\tanh a=\frac8{17},\quad\operatorname{sech}a=\frac{15}{17}',r'\operatorname{cosech}a=\frac{15}{8},\quad\operatorname{coth}a=\frac{17}{8}')+'<p>Cosh is positive, so the positive square root is compulsory. Take the reciprocals only after finding the correct ratios.</p>'),
 ex('E3','3 · Keep both logarithmic roots',r'<p>Solve \(6\cosh x-4\sinh x=7\) for every real x.</p>',
 D(r'e^x+5e^{-x}=7',r'u=e^x>0:\quad u^2-7u+5=0',r'u=\frac{7\pm\sqrt{29}}2',r'x=\ln\left(\frac{7+\sqrt{29}}2\right)\quad\text{or}\quad x=\ln\left(\frac{7-\sqrt{29}}2\right)')+'<p>Both u-roots are positive because √29&lt;7. Each gives one real x; neither may be discarded.</p>'),
 ex('E4','4 · Read a minimum from a shifted function',r'<p>Find the minimum of \(5e^x+20e^{-x}\), and solve \(5e^x+20e^{-x}=25\).</p>',
 D(r'5e^x+20e^{-x}=20\cosh(x-\ln2)',r'\min=20\quad\text{at }x=\ln2',r'\cosh(x-\ln2)=\frac54',r'x=0\quad\text{or}\quad x=\ln4')+'<p>The shift locates the minimum. Cosh takes the value 5/4 at the two arguments ±ln2, so both intersections are needed.</p>')])
lesson('graphs','Graphs, ranges and transformations','Track support, symmetry and unattained asymptotic values.',
 'Describe transformed hyperbolic graphs and determine exact graph features.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'exponential">definitions</a>. Review translations, stretches, reflections and open or closed interval endpoints.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Shape and real ranges</h3><p>Sinh increases through the origin with all-real domain and range. Cosh is U-shaped, symmetric about the vertical axis, and has minimum 1. Tanh increases between horizontal asymptotes −1 and 1, neither of which it reaches. Sech is positive with maximum 1 and tends to zero in both tails. Cosech is odd with vertical asymptote x=0 and horizontal asymptote y=0. Coth is odd, excludes x=0, and has horizontal asymptotes ±1.</p>'''+D(r'\operatorname{range}(\cosh)=[1,\infty)',r'\operatorname{range}(\tanh)=(-1,1)',r'\operatorname{range}(\operatorname{sech})=(0,1]',r'\operatorname{range}(\operatorname{cosech})=(-\infty,0)\cup(0,\infty)',r'\operatorname{range}(\operatorname{coth})=(-\infty,-1)\cup(1,\infty)')+r'''<h3>Transform the input first</h3><p>For y=A f(B(x−h))+D, a base point (r,s) maps to (h+r/B,D+As). Negative A reflects vertically; negative B reflects horizontally. A finite turning point is attained and included in the range. A finite horizontal asymptote is a limit and may remain excluded.</p><h3>Describe the whole sketch</h3><p>State the domain, range, exact intercepts, symmetry and asymptotes. Solve the argument equation to locate a vertical asymptote. Check the sign on each side instead of drawing a branch through an excluded input. A flat-looking inverse-sinh tail is not a horizontal asymptote.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>Identify the base function and its real domain.</li><li>Map important points and symmetry under the transformation.</li><li>Calculate asymptotes by limits and excluded arguments.</li><li>Solve for all intercepts with exact values.</li><li>State the range with correct endpoint inclusion.</li></ol>',
 ['A shifted vertical asymptote is found from the argument, not from y=0.','Horizontal asymptotes of tanh and coth are not attained.','A negative multiplier reverses graph direction.','Sech reaches 1 but never reaches 0.','Do not join branches across a forbidden input.'],[
 ex('G1','1 · A reflected sine-hyperbolic curve',r'<p>Describe \(y=1-3\sinh(x+2)\): symmetry, direction, range and horizontal intercept.</p>',
 D(r'\text{centre}=(-2,1)',r'\text{domain}=\mathbb R,\qquad\text{range}=\mathbb R',r'\sinh(x+2)=\frac13',r'x=\ln\left(\frac{1+\sqrt{10}}3\right)-2')+'<p>The vertical reflection makes the curve decrease. Its central point shifts left by 2 and up by 1. There are no horizontal or vertical asymptotes.</p>'),
 ex('G2','2 · A cosh minimum and two zeros',r'<p>Describe the minimum, range and zeros of \(y=2\cosh(2x-2)-3\).</p>',
 D(r'\text{minimum point}=(1,-1)',r'\text{range}=[-1,\infty)',r'\cosh(2x-2)=\frac32',r'x=1\pm\frac12\ln\left(\frac{3+\sqrt5}2\right)')+'<p>The argument vanishes at x=1. The positive multiplier keeps this a minimum. The two zeros lie symmetrically on opposite sides of x=1.</p>'),
 ex('G3','3 · A decreasing bounded curve',r'<p>Find the range, asymptotes and centre of \(y=4-2\tanh(x-3)\). Does it cross the horizontal axis?</p>',
 D(r'-1<\tanh(x-3)<1',r'2<y<6',r'\text{asymptotes: }y=2,\ y=6',r'\text{centre}=(3,4)')+'<p>The curve decreases from values near 6 to values near 2. Neither endpoint is attained, and y is always positive, so there is no horizontal-axis intersection.</p>'),
 ex('G4','4 · Move a reciprocal singularity',r'<p>Find the domain, range, asymptotes and zero of \(y=\operatorname{coth}(2x-1)+2\).</p>',
 D(r'x\ne\frac12',r'\text{range}=(-\infty,1)\cup(3,\infty)',r'\text{asymptotes: }x=\frac12,\ y=1,\ y=3',r'\operatorname{coth}(2x-1)=-2',r'x=\frac12-\frac14\ln3')+'<p>The zero lies on the branch to the left of the vertical asymptote. The lower branch ends towards y=1; the upper branch tends towards y=3.</p>')])
lesson('identities','Identities and admissible solutions','Prove identities from definitions, then preserve all permitted branches.',
 'Derive and use hyperbolic identities with the restrictions needed for equivalence.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'graphs">graphs and ranges</a>. Review expanding powers and solving polynomial equations.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Prove a general statement before substituting numbers</h3><p>Replace the functions by their exponential definitions, expand one side, and simplify towards the other. State restrictions when a denominator can vanish. A numerical example can check an identity, but is not its proof.</p>'''+D(r'\sinh(u+v)=\sinh u\cosh v+\cosh u\sinh v',r'\cosh(u+v)=\cosh u\cosh v+\sinh u\sinh v',r'\sinh2x=2\sinh x\cosh x',r'\cosh2x=2\cosh^2x-1=1+2\sinh^2x',r'\tanh2x=\frac{2\tanh x}{1+\tanh^2x}')+r'''<h3>Polynomial roots need function ranges</h3><p>If s=sinh x, then every real s is allowed and gives exactly one x. If c=cosh x, require c≥1; c=1 gives x=0, while c&gt;1 gives two opposite roots. If t=tanh x, require −1&lt;t&lt;1. Do not divide by s or t before considering its zero value.</p><h3>Count horizontal intersections</h3><p>A discriminant counts polynomial roots before these restrictions. To classify a parameter problem, first identify the admissible variable range and boundary values. Include equality cases separately.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>Choose exponential definitions or a proved identity.</li><li>Record every denominator restriction.</li><li>Substitute s=sinh x, c=cosh x or t=tanh x with its range.</li><li>Solve and discard only inadmissible substituted roots.</li><li>Recover all corresponding x-values, including zero and both cosh branches.</li></ol>',
 ['Hyperbolic addition formulae have different signs from circular identities.','Keep the zero root before cancelling a factor.','A real quadratic root below 1 cannot be cosh x.','A small discriminant does not count admissible x-roots.','Restrictions belong to the original expression as well as the simplified one.'],[
 ex('H1','1 · Establish the double-angle formula',r'<p>Prove \(\sinh2x=2\sinh x\cosh x\) from exponential definitions.</p>',
 D(r'2\sinh x\cosh x=\frac{(e^x-e^{-x})(e^x+e^{-x})}{2}',r'=\frac{e^{2x}-e^{-2x}}2=\sinh2x')+'<p>The cross terms cancel. No division was needed, so the result holds for every real x, including zero.</p>'),
 ex('H2','2 · A root that cancellation would lose',r'<p>Solve \(\tanh2x=\frac85\tanh x\).</p>',
 D(r't=\tanh x,\quad -1<t<1',r'\frac{2t}{1+t^2}=\frac85t',r't(1-4t^2)=0',r't=0,\quad t=\pm\frac12',r'x=0,\quad x=\pm\frac12\ln3')+'<p>The denominator is positive. All three substituted values lie in the tanh range, and each corresponds to one real x.</p>'),
 ex('H3','3 · A triple angle with two branches',r'<p>Use \(\cosh3x=4\cosh^3x-3\cosh x\) to solve \(\cosh3x=13\cosh x\).</p>',
 D(r'4\cosh x(\cosh^2x-4)=0',r'\cosh x=2',r'x=\pm\ln(2+\sqrt3)')+'<p>Cosh is positive, so its factor cannot vanish. The negative candidate −2 is outside its range; the positive value 2 gives two opposite x-values.</p>'),
 ex('H4','4 · An identity locates a minimum',r'<p>Write \(25\cosh x-7\sinh x\) as a shifted cosh and find its minimum.</p>',
 D(r'R^2=25^2-7^2=576,\qquad R=24',r'\cosh b=\frac{25}{24},\quad\sinh b=\frac7{24}',r'e^b=\frac43,\quad b=\ln\frac43',r'25\cosh x-7\sinh x=24\cosh\left(x-\ln\frac43\right)')+'<p>The minimum is 24, attained only at x=ln(4/3). Matching both exponential coefficients verifies the shift, rather than just matching the minimum.</p>')])
lesson('inverse','Inverse functions and principal branches','An inverse undoes a one-to-one function; it is not a reciprocal.',
 'Find inverse formulae, domains and ranges using the correct principal branch.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'identities">identities</a>. Review one-to-one restrictions and reflection in y=x.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Three standard real inverses</h3>'''+D(r'\sinh:\mathbb R\longrightarrow\mathbb R',r'\cosh:[0,\infty)\longrightarrow[1,\infty)',r'\tanh:\mathbb R\longrightarrow(-1,1)')+r'''<p>Sinh and tanh are already one-to-one. Cosh needs a restriction; its principal inverse returns a non-negative value. The inverse domain equals the restricted function's range, and the inverse range equals its restricted domain.</p>'''+D(r'\sinh^{-1}(\sinh x)=x',r'\tanh^{-1}(\tanh x)=x',r'\cosh^{-1}(\cosh x)=|x|')+r'''<p>The reverse compositions require the input to lie in the inverse domain. The notation sinh⁻¹x means inverse sinh, not 1/sinh x. Reciprocals retain the names cosech, sech and coth.</p><h3>Transformed inverse functions</h3><p>Write y=f(x), isolate the base hyperbolic function and impose its branch before inverting. Interchange variable names only at the end. Keep all affine shifts and stretches. Reflection in y=x exchanges horizontal and vertical graph features.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>Specify a one-to-one branch for the original function.</li><li>Find its exact range.</li><li>Rearrange y=f(x) and apply the principal inverse.</li><li>Interchange names to obtain f⁻¹.</li><li>State inverse domain and range and test the requested values.</li></ol>',
 ['Inverse notation and reciprocal notation have different meanings.','Principal inverse cosh has a non-negative output.','Cosh⁻¹(cosh x) equals |x|, not x everywhere.','The inverse domain is the original range.','Carry every affine transformation through the inversion.'],[
 ex('V1','1 · Choose the least increasing branch',r'<p>Restrict \(f(x)=\cosh(4x-3)\) to an increasing branch, then find its inverse and both inverse intervals.</p>',
 D(r'x\ge\frac34',r'f^{-1}(x)=\frac{\cosh^{-1}x+3}{4}',r'\operatorname{domain}(f^{-1})=[1,\infty)',r'\operatorname{range}(f^{-1})=[3/4,\infty)')+'<p>The argument is non-negative precisely on this branch. Applying inverse cosh there produces 4x−3 directly, with no ± sign.</p>'),
 ex('V2','2 · Invert a bounded transformed function',r'<p>For \(f(x)=5\tanh(x-2)-3\), find the inverse, its domain and its range.</p>',
 D(r'y+3=5\tanh(x-2)',r'x=2+\tanh^{-1}\left(\frac{y+3}{5}\right)',r'f^{-1}(x)=2+\tanh^{-1}\left(\frac{x+3}{5}\right)',r'\operatorname{domain}(f^{-1})=(-8,2)',r'\operatorname{range}(f^{-1})=\mathbb R')+'<p>The original output lies strictly between −8 and 2, so neither endpoint is admitted to the inverse domain.</p>'),
 ex('V3','3 · The modulus determines both roots',r'<p>Solve \(\cosh^{-1}(\cosh(3x+4))=2\).</p>',
 D(r'|3x+4|=2',r'3x+4=2\quad\text{or}\quad3x+4=-2',r'x=-\frac23\quad\text{or}\quad x=-2')+'<p>Both signs are needed because principal inverse cosh returns the magnitude of the inner real argument. Substitution confirms both roots.</p>'),
 ex('V4','4 · Compose in the permitted direction',r'<p>Simplify \(\cosh(\sinh^{-1}z)\) and \(\tanh(\sinh^{-1}z)\), then solve the second expression equal to \(\frac5{13}\).</p>',
 D(r'\cosh(\sinh^{-1}z)=\sqrt{1+z^2}',r'\tanh(\sinh^{-1}z)=\frac{z}{\sqrt{1+z^2}}',r'144z^2=25,\qquad z>0',r'z=\frac5{12}')+'<p>The positive square root is forced by cosh. The original equation fixes a positive sign, so the negative root introduced by squaring is rejected.</p>')])
lesson('logarithms','Logarithmic forms and exact equations','Derive a formula and justify the branch and domain at the same time.',
 'Derive the three main logarithmic inverse forms and solve equations without extraneous roots.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'inverse">inverse functions</a>. Review quadratic roots, logarithm laws and their positive arguments.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Solve for a positive exponential</h3><p>To derive an inverse formula, set y equal to the inverse and write the corresponding forward relation. Substituting u=eʸ turns the exponential relation into a quadratic or linear equation. Select u&gt;0 and, for inverse cosh, u≥1.</p>'''+D(r'\sinh^{-1}x=\ln\left(x+\sqrt{x^2+1}\right),\quad x\in\mathbb R',r'\cosh^{-1}x=\ln\left(x+\sqrt{x^2-1}\right),\quad x\ge1',r'\tanh^{-1}x=\frac12\ln\frac{1+x}{1-x},\quad -1<x<1')+r'''<p>For inverse sinh, √(x²+1)&gt;|x| makes the plus root positive for every real input. For inverse cosh, the plus root is at least 1 and gives the non-negative principal output. For inverse tanh, the quotient must be positive on its real inverse domain.</p><h3>Reciprocal inverses follow from the same functions</h3>'''+D(r'\operatorname{coth}^{-1}x=\tanh^{-1}(1/x),\quad |x|>1',r'\operatorname{sech}^{-1}x=\cosh^{-1}(1/x),\quad 0<x\le1',r'\operatorname{cosech}^{-1}x=\sinh^{-1}(1/x),\quad x\ne0')+r'''<p>Here inverse sech uses the non-negative branch. The reciprocal inside an inverse does not turn inverse notation into reciprocal notation.</p><h3>Preserve equivalence in an equation</h3><p>Intersect all real domains before combining logarithms, squaring or applying a forward function. Cosh is one-to-one only on a restricted half-line. At the end check every candidate in the original domain; a valid polynomial root may still be invalid for an inverse expression.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>Write the original real domain.</li><li>Use the exponential definition or a justified logarithmic inverse form.</li><li>Keep positive logarithm arguments and the required principal branch.</li><li>Solve the algebraic equation.</li><li>Retain every candidate in the original domain and check it.</li></ol>',
 ['The logarithm requires a positive argument.','Inverse cosh chooses a non-negative output.','Combining inverse tanh terms needs the intersection of their domains.','Squaring can introduce the wrong sign.','Do not present a reciprocal as an inverse function.'],[
 ex('L1','1 · Derive inverse sinh for every real input',r'<p>Derive a logarithmic expression for \(\sinh^{-1}x\), with its real domain.</p>',
 D(r'x=\sinh y,\quad u=e^y>0',r'u^2-2xu-1=0',r'u=x\pm\sqrt{x^2+1}',r'\sinh^{-1}x=\ln\left(x+\sqrt{x^2+1}\right)')+'<p>The minus root is negative because √(x²+1)&gt;|x|. The plus root is strictly positive for every real x, so the displayed formula has all-real domain.</p>'),
 ex('L2','2 · Derive inverse cosh on its principal branch',r'<p>Derive the logarithmic expression for principal \(\cosh^{-1}x\).</p>',
 D(r'x=\cosh y,\quad y\ge0,\quad u=e^y\ge1',r'u^2-2xu+1=0',r'u=x\pm\sqrt{x^2-1}',r'\cosh^{-1}x=\ln\left(x+\sqrt{x^2-1}\right),\quad x\ge1')+'<p>For x&gt;1 the two roots are positive reciprocals. Only the plus root is at least 1, as the principal branch requires. At x=1 both roots equal 1 and y=0.</p>'),
 ex('L3','3 · Derive inverse tanh and its open domain',r'<p>Derive a logarithmic expression for \(\tanh^{-1}x\).</p>',
 D(r'x=\frac{e^{2y}-1}{e^{2y}+1}',r'e^{2y}=\frac{1+x}{1-x}',r'\tanh^{-1}x=\frac12\ln\frac{1+x}{1-x},\quad -1<x<1')+'<p>The real inverse domain comes from the open range of tanh. The two endpoints would make the logarithm invalid or unbounded, and are excluded.</p>'),
 ex('L4','4 · Reject a root outside both inverse domains',r'<p>Solve \(\tanh^{-1}x+\tanh^{-1}(3x)=\frac12\ln7\).</p>',
 D(r'-\frac13<x<\frac13',r'\frac{(1+x)(1+3x)}{(1-x)(1-3x)}=7',r'9x^2-16x+3=0',r'x=\frac{8\pm\sqrt{37}}9',r'x=\frac{8-\sqrt{37}}9')+'<p>Only the smaller root lies in the common domain. All logarithm arguments are positive there, so the transformations are reversible and this is the complete solution.</p>')])
lesson('mixed','Mixed problems and later-topic extensions','Classify core equations completely; attempt extensions only after their prerequisites.',
 'Combine range restrictions, parameter cases and principal branches, then review optional later-topic applications.',
 '<p>Complete all five preceding lessons. Questions 41–45 are chapter-core review. Questions 46–50 are later-topic extensions: attempt them after differentiation, integration and first-order differential equations. The last two worked examples also need these later prerequisites.</p>',
 r'''<h3>A parameter changes the shape and the number of roots</h3><p>In A cosh x−b sinh x=k, the cases |b|&lt;A, |b|=A and |b|&gt;A behave differently when A&gt;0. The first case has a positive minimum and up to two roots. Equality leaves a single positive exponential. In the last case the exponential coefficients have opposite signs, giving a strictly monotone function with all-real range.</p><h3>Substitutions carry ranges with them</h3>'''+D(r'c=\cosh x\ge1,\qquad s=\sinh x\in\mathbb R',r't=\tanh(x/2)\in(-1,1)',r'\sinh x=\frac{2t}{1-t^2},\qquad\cosh x=\frac{1+t^2}{1-t^2}')+r'''<p>Positive x corresponds to positive sinh x. A repeated polynomial root gives one distinct substituted value, whose corresponding x-values still depend on the function. For radical inverse equations, keep the non-negative branches before applying cosh.</p><h3>Later-topic extension: verify a proposed solution</h3><p>After learning integration and differential equations, a hyperbolic integrating factor can simplify a linear equation. Substitute the final expression into the original equation, check the initial condition and state the interval where the steps are valid. An endpoint limit need not be a solution through that endpoint.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>Identify whether the task is core review or a later-topic extension.</li><li>Record the domain and every special parameter case.</li><li>Use a range-preserving substitution or principal inverse.</li><li>Count all distinct real roots, including equality boundaries.</li><li>For an extension, verify the differential equation, initial value and stated interval.</li></ol>',
 ['A discriminant does not impose the range c≥1.','Special parameters can turn a quadratic into a linear equation.','A positive x-root needs a positive sinh x-value.','Applying cosh is not globally one-to-one.','Later-topic differential equations require their own prerequisites and domain checks.'],[
 ex('M1','1 · Include the exponential boundary cases',r'<p>For real a, classify the number of real roots of \(2\cosh x-a\sinh x=k\).</p>',
 D(r'|a|<2:\quad \min=\sqrt{4-a^2}',r'|a|=2:\quad F(x)=2e^x\ \text{or}\ 2e^{-x}',r'|a|>2:\quad \operatorname{range}(F)=\mathbb R')+'<p>For |a|&lt;2 there are zero, one or two roots as k is below, equal to or above the minimum. For |a|=2 there is one root for k&gt;0 and none otherwise. For |a|&gt;2 the function is strictly monotone and gives exactly one root for each real k.</p>'),
 ex('M2','2 · An inverse equation with a radical',r'<p>Solve \(\cosh^{-1}(2x+5)=2\sinh^{-1}\sqrt{3x}\).</p>',
 D(r'x\ge0',r'2x+5=\cosh\left(2\sinh^{-1}\sqrt{3x}\right)=1+6x',r'x=1')+'<p>Both original sides are non-negative on the common domain. Cosh is one-to-one there, so the transformation is reversible. The candidate x=1 satisfies the original equation and is unique.</p>'),
 ex('M3','3 · Extension after first-order differential equations',r'<p>After the later prerequisites, solve \(y^\prime+2y\tanh x=1\), with \(y(0)=3\), and find the limit as x→∞.</p>',
 D(r'\mu(x)=\cosh^2x',r'(y\cosh^2x)^\prime=\cosh^2x',r'y=\frac{\frac x2+\frac14\sinh2x+3}{\cosh^2x}',r'\lim_{x\to\infty}y=\frac12')+'<p>The integrating factor is positive everywhere. The initial value sets the constant to 3. Rewriting the sinh term as ½tanh x shows the limit; the remaining linear term decays after multiplication by sech²x.</p>'),
 ex('M4','4 · Extension with an explicit interval',r'<p>For x&gt;−4, solve \((x+4)y^\prime-y=(x+4)^2\sinh^{-1}((x+4)/2)\), with \(y(0)=0\).</p>',
 D(r'u=x+4,\qquad v=\sinh^{-1}(u/2)',r'\left(\frac yu\right)^\prime=\sinh^{-1}(u/2)',r'\frac yu=uv-\sqrt{u^2+4}+C',r'C=2\sqrt5-4\sinh^{-1}2')+'<p>Here u=x+4, so du/dx=1. Multiply the final right-hand side by u to obtain y explicitly. Integration by parts supplies the displayed antiderivative. Differentiating y/u verifies the equation; at x=0 the bracket is zero. The restriction x&gt;−4 keeps the integrating-factor step valid.</p>')])
